    `Father, I close this week at the invitation — the simplest and the most complete word available to me. Come. All I am and all I carry: the weight of the goodness I am still waiting to see, the cost of the presence I have been offering, the sustained engagement of the prayer framework, the school of contentment I am still in, the wakefulness to the hour that the darkness makes difficult, and the wounds I am still carrying. All of it comes with me. And You say: come to me, all you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest. I come. Not with the impressive portfolio of spiritual achievement. With the weariness and the burden and the genuine need of a person who has been in the week and is still carrying what they were carrying when it began. And I receive what You promise: rest. Not the end of the journey. The refreshment of a person who has been in the yoke with You, whose burden has been easy and light precisely because Your shoulder has been under the other side. I come. Again. As always. Because this is the posture — the ongoing, repeated, daily return to the One who gives rest every time I come and has never once turned away the weary and the burdened who arrived. Come. I am coming. Here I am.`
